<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Massasoit</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1581–<a href="/years/1661">1661</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Ousamequin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Massasoit, also called Ousamequin, was the sachem, or paramount leader, of the Wampanoag in the early 17th century, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sowams">Sowams</a> in present-day Rhode Island. In March 1621 he made a treaty of mutual defence with the Pilgrims of Plymouth Colony, and that autumn he shared the harvest feast later remembered as the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thanksgiving">Thanksgiving</a>. He kept the peace with the English until his death around 1661.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Massasoit means Great Sachem. It was his title, but the English colonists took it for his name and it stuck. When the Pilgrims arrived, the realm of the Wampanoag, also called the Pokanokets, took in parts of Rhode Island and much of south-eastern Massachusetts, and lesser Pokanoket sachems owed him allegiance. Outbreaks of an unidentified disease had devastated his people and left them exposed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narragansett-people">Narragansetts</a>, who held the land west of Narragansett Bay.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Seeking allies against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narragansett-people">Narragansetts</a>, Massasoit sent Samoset, a minor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abenaki">Abenaki</a> sachem from Maine who had learned English from fishermen, to find out whether the colonists meant peace, and in 1621 he sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisquantum">Squanto</a> to live among them. The peace treaty of 22 March 1621 bound him to William Bradford, Edward Winslow, Stephen Hopkins, John Carver and Myles Standish. According to English sources, his help saved Plymouth Colony from failure and starvation in its first years, and the alliance kept the Pokanokets neutral in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pequot-war">Pequot War</a> in 1636.</p><p>Tension rose when the colonists refused to give up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tisquantum">Squanto</a>, whom Massasoit believed had betrayed him, but it was resolved in March 1623 when Winslow nursed the gravely ill sachem back to health. Massasoit then warned the English of a plan by Massachusett warriors to destroy the Wessagusset and Plymouth colonies. He sheltered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-williams">Roger Williams</a> in the winter of 1635-36 and gave him land on the Seekonk River. As settlement spread, he sold a tract 14 miles square to Myles Standish and others of Duxbury in 1649, at Sachem Rock in East Bridgewater.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Massasoit had five children: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wamsutta">Wamsutta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metacom">Metacom</a>, Sonkanuchoo, Amie and Sarah. After his death Wamsutta and Metacom asked Plymouth for English names and were called Alexander and Philip. Wamsutta, who succeeded his father and turned to an alliance with Connecticut Colony, died within a year; Metacom, who followed him in 1662, believed the colonists had murdered him, one cause of King Philip's War, which only Amie of the five children survived. Statues of Massasoit by Cyrus E. Dallin stand near Plymouth Rock and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> and Missouri.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Massasoit” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The settlers of Plymouth Colony under Governor John Carver made a treaty of mutual defence with <b>Massasoit</b>, sachem of the Wampanoag, with Tisquantum interpreting.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-22">22 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pilgrims survived with help from the Wampanoag Confederacy, whose sachem <b>Massasoit</b> made an alliance with them in 1621.</blockquote><a href="/state/plymouth-colony">Plymouth Colony</a></li><li><blockquote>A devastating epidemic in 1616 to 1619 killed a large share of them shortly before the Pilgrims arrived, and the sachem <b>Massasoit</b>, ruling from Sowams, held the communities together.</blockquote><a href="/state/wampanoag-confederacy">Wampanoag Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
